            <div xml:id="d314">

                <pb facs="0930" n="889" sameAs="frus:frus1977-80v24/pg889"/>

                <head>314. Telegram From the White House to the Embassy in Peru<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d314-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1980-05-27T19:21:00+00:00">May 27, 1980, 1921Z</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>White House 80613. For Ambassador Shlaudeman</p>
                <p>Please deliver the following letter from the President to President Morales
                    Bermudez at the earliest opportunity.</p>
                <pb facs="0931" n="890" xml:id="pg890"/>
                <p>Begin Text:</p>
                <p>Dear Mr. President:</p>
                <p>Assistant Secretary of State Mathea Falco has reported on a recent visit to Peru
                    and on the impressive efforts your government is making to combat the flow of
                    illicit drugs.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d314-f2"/></p>
                <p>The emergency law passed earlier this year aimed at eliminating illicit coca
                    production in the key growing areas of Peru is, in our view, a vital step in
                    dealing with the problem. The subsequent “Green Sea II” operation enforcing that
                    law has produced dramatic and positive results.</p>
                <p>You are, of course, aware of the United States’ strong domestic and international
                    commitment to curbing illicit drug traffic. We see that traffic both as a threat
                    to our own society and to the society of nations. The crime and enormous flows
                    of money that it generates can undermine the economies. The political
                    structures, and the national security of the producing countries.</p>
                <p>For these reasons, I take great satisfaction in the close cooperation our two
                    countries have developed in meeting this difficult problem.</p>
                <p>With warmest wishes,</p>
                <p>Sincerely,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#bold">Jimmy
                            Carter</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d314-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d314-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Carter Library, National Security Affairs, Brzezinski Material, President’s Correspondence with Foreign
                    Leaders, Box 16, Peru: President Francisco Morales
                        Bermudez Cerutti, 5/77-6/80. Confidential. Sent for information
                    to the Department of State.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d314-f2" n="2" xml:id="d314-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram 2940 from Lima, April 2, the Embassy reported on Falco’s trip.
                    (National Archives, RG 59, Central Foreign Policy File, D800174-0353)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
